    <iq:application entry="TempustApp" id="1de83b78-c0ce-40c0-b717-0f98fdb37f48" launcherIcon="@Drawables.LauncherIcon" minSdkVersion="3.1.8" name="@Strings.AppName" type="widget" version="1.0.0">
        <!--
            NOTE: replace the placeholder "id" above with the real UUID
            that the Connect IQ SDK generates automatically when you
            create the project via "Connect IQ: New Project" in VS Code
            (see README).
        -->
        <!--
            minSdkVersion is 3.1.8, not the SDK's newer default -
            that's the exact API level this app's tightest real
            dependency needs (Graphics.FONT_GLANCE, used by the glance
            view). Everything else this app uses (Position, Timer,
            Communications, App.Properties, GlanceView itself at
            3.1.0) is available at or below that level, so 3.1.8 is
            the lowest value that's actually safe - not a arbitrary
            pick.

            IMPORTANT, if you ever change minSdkVersion: this app
            mixes an optional (:glance) getGlanceView() override into
            TempustApp, the app's entry point class. On this SDK
            (9.2.0), setting minSdkVersion below GlanceView's own
            minimum (3.1.0) doesn't just drop glance support - it can
            break the ENTIRE entry point on some devices with an
            unrelated-looking "Cannot find entry point class" or
            "Undefined symbol" error (confirmed by testing 3.0.0 and
            3.2.0 against this exact codebase; only versions in
            GlanceView's actual supported range, like this one, built
            cleanly everywhere). If you lower this value, retest a
            broad device sample individually (-d <device>), not just
            the device your simulator happens to be running, and don't
            assume a clean build stays clean at every value.
        -->
        <!--
            Every device in the installed SDK's device database that
            (a) supports the Widget app type and (b) actually builds
            against minSdkVersion above - checked by compiling
            individually for each one, not guessed. That's the
            practical definition of "reasonable Garmin devices for
            this kind of app" used here: this widget needs GPS +
            network access + a way to appear on the device, and every
            device meeting all three ended up on this list - watches,
            handheld GPS units (GPSMAP, Montana, eTrex Touch), and bike
            computers (Edge) alike, since ambient temperature is
            useful on all of them.

            fenix 9 (43mm/47mm, Pro 43/47/51mm, Pro Solar 47/51mm)
            added 2026-09 once the installed SDK's device database
            picked them up - the SDK Manager can refresh that database
            in place (no new SDK version folder) ahead of a full SDK
            release, so a device rejected as "invalid" one day can
            become valid the next without anything in this project
            changing. Re-verified by compiling for each fenix 9 id
            individually, same as everything else here, not assumed
            from the SDK Manager's device catalog alone (that catalog
            can know a product exists before the compiler itself
            supports building for it).

            Excluded, and why:
            - Devices without Widget support at all (mostly Garmin's
              non-GPS fitness bands - vivomove/vivosmart/vivofit/Lily -
              which don't offer Widget as an app type in the first
              place, so they were never candidates).
            - Devices whose firmware doesn't reach API level 3.1.8:
              fenix3/5(base)/6(pre-Pro on some sizes)/Chronos, several
              early Approach/D2/Edge/Forerunner/GPSMAP/vivoactive
              models, etc. - confirmed by compiling for each one
              individually, not by version-number guessing.
            - "bounce2" doesn't exist as a compiler-recognized product
              id in this SDK despite having a device reference page -
              left out rather than guessing at an alternate id.

            Garmin adds new watches and occasionally tweaks product-id
            strings over time, and the SDK validates every id against
            its own device database at build time (an unknown id fails
            the build immediately, so nothing here can go stale
            silently in that sense) - but devices released after this
            SDK version obviously won't be in this list yet. Before
            publishing, open this project in the VS Code Monkey C
            extension and use "Edit Compatible Devices" to check
            against the current catalog.
        -->
